A||||16||fiero56||3.2 crank???||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||03-15-2000||08:50 PM||||I was running my usuall e-bay haunt, and found this ad.  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=282676026" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=282676026</A> <P>First off, I don't plan on getting one of his kits. Second, he mentions slapping in a 3.2 crank and assorted, instead of a 3.1. Whats this, I've never heard of it working that way. Is he wrong, or is it possible to put in a 3.2 crank? I highly doubt a 3.2 crank is a direct plug in.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric||03-15-2000||09:40 PM||||I think he means 3.1 crank.  He probably got the 3.2 from the extra displacement of the .040 over pistons.  I think his intent was to keep things simple and not to confuse anybody.  Doesn't sound like plan worked to well.<P>You may be able to do better on price and component quality if you were to do the shopping yourself.  I personally would want a bigger than stock cam and maybe Felpro gaskets. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-15-2000||09:53 PM||||If/when I ever do the rebuild, I plan to completely redo the engine, and use high quality parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87SC/GT||03-15-2000||10:26 PM||||That does not seem like a very good deal to me. Check out <A HREF="http://www.naparts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.naparts.com/</A>  . I know they had a complete kit ( less crank) for like 350.00 . They also have 3.1 cranks on an exchange basis.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||03-16-2000||05:38 AM||||If you have a pre 88 motor you also need a different flex plate/flywheel.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fastonpumpgas||03-17-2000||06:07 PM||||WARNING:<BR>   I am doing the 3.2 converstion, it is not as easy as everyone says it to be.  Do lots of research first, adn i wish people would stop calling it a 3.2, DO the math ......Definately dont forget a 2,8 is externaly balanced and an 3.1 is interal, 350 bucks will get you the parts (pistons , ring and cranks) (( although mine were alot more)) to all those who want to venture into this... GOOD LUCK, I should have it running and dynoed by may||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||03-17-2000||08:59 PM||||I can attest to the project not being easy.  It took quite a few months to get all the right parts, but I don't think a quality 2.8 rebuild would be much easier.<P>I was under the impression that a 1mm overbore would give the 3.1 engine 1/10 of a liter more displacement, therefore making it a 3.2.  What is the acutual #?  Don't forget we have to round up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phaeton||03-17-2000||09:47 PM||||90 mm bore x 84 mm stroke = 3.20631 liters.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||03-17-2000||09:56 PM||||Am I confused on something here? I too, was going with the the thinking a 1mm overbore, along with the 3.1 crank gave 3.2 liters of displacement. If my math is incorrect, I would like to know.<P>Also, was the '88 2.8 not internally balanced?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||03-18-2000||06:56 PM||||56, you have a formula right?  If so, you can use your original flywheel.  I have an 88GT and I just got my flywheel resurfaced.  3.1 3.2, it makes very little difference in the real world.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||03-18-2000||07:55 PM||||56, Yes. 88s are internally balanced.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||03-19-2000||12:07 AM||||Yes, I have an '88 Formula. I am going to get my initial planning started soon, cams, cranks, rockers, etc. So I am sure I will have quite a few questions in the months ahead.<BR>I may not know much when I start a project, but I have yet to have that stop me. All I can say is research, research, and more research.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||pherder||03-19-2000||10:33 AM||||Well, I was lucky, I guess      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I got a 93 Chevy Lumina APV 3.1 (yes, it has the cast iron heads!) w/30K miles on it for $400~<P>I am currently swapping the Fiero's pieces onto it and getting it ready to plug in.<P>So far I have <BR>$400~ in the engine, <BR>$300~ in head work (3.1 heads were damaged in the accident that put it in the yard so I am using my 2.8 heads ... they were the same except for the damage ... only tireder) <BR>$160 in the Crane cam, <BR>$110 in powder coating, <BR>$165 in a ported set of stock Fiero exhaust manifolds, <BR>$280 in HPC coating, and about<BR>$100~ in extras (plugs, oil, filters, PCV, gaskets, etc.)<P>YOOOO!!!!!!  $1515         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I wish I hadn't figured that up ...<P>Well, it looks good!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>    <IMG SRC=""http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View=208525&a=1537450&p=17097103&Sequence=0"">  <P>Well it would if this !@#$ program would show it!  Goto:  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=208525&a=1537450&p=17097103&Sequence=0" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=208525&a=1537450&p=17097103&Sequence=0</A>  to see it since I can't make it show up here!<P>Oh, well!  I have a lot more in my Quad 4 swap and will have way too much in my V8 swap.<P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Eric||03-19-2000||11:26 AM||||The Fiero heads are better anyway.  ||||reg||14
Z||000015||pherder||03-19-2000||05:00 PM||||EVERY measurement I can make tells me they are the same!<P>If you can tell me what is different, I will check it out and report back to the group.<P>I do know that the pistons are different (more of a dish).<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Eric||03-19-2000||05:28 PM||||I thought the non HO (non Fiero) heads had smaller exhaust valves than the Fiero heads.  What do you mean by dished pistons?  The aftermarket pistons I got for my rebuild were dished, are you saying the minivan's pistons have an even deeper dish?||||reg||1
Z||000017||pherder||03-19-2000||09:36 PM||||Eric,<P>The 3.1 heads have the exact size valves as the Fiero's 2.8<P>There are two different, cast iron head pistons available.  One for the Camaro and the other for the mini-vans.  The mini-vans have a deeper dish (i.e. lower compression)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I don't think it is much but worth making sure you get the correct pistons.  <P>There is the aluminum head pistons also, but I haven't seen a picture of those and I know they will not work with the cast iron heads.<P>Paul||||reg||1
